<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Fa22-Midterm1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math 234 - Midterm 1 - Fall 2022</title>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Let <m>\vec{a}=\langle 2,4,0\rangle</m> and <m>\vec{b}=\langle 3,4,1\rangle</m>. Find the vector projection of <m>\vec{b}</m> onto <m>\vec{a}</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				The vector projection of <m>\vec{b}</m> onto <m>\vec{a}</m> is given by:
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{a}} \vec{b} \amp = \frac{\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b}}{|\vec{a}|^2} \vec{a} \\
					\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} \amp = 2(3) + 4(4) + 0(1) = 22 \\
					|\vec{a}|^2 \amp = 2^2 + 4^2 + 0^2 = 20 \\
					\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{a}} \vec{b} \amp = \frac{22}{20} \langle 2, 4, 0 \rangle = \frac{11}{10} \langle 2, 4, 0 \rangle = \langle 2.2, 4.4, 0 \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find the equation of the plane that contains the points <m>A(1,0,2), B(0,1,1)</m>, and <m>C(2,3,-1)</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				First, we find two vectors in the plane:
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\overrightarrow{AB} \amp = \langle 0-1, 1-0, 1-2 \rangle = \langle -1, 1, -1 \rangle \\
					\overrightarrow{AC} \amp = \langle 2-1, 3-0, -1-2 \rangle = \langle 1, 3, -3 \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				The normal vector is the cross product:
				<me>
					\vec{n} = \overrightarrow{AB} \times \overrightarrow{AC} = \begin{vmatrix} \hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\ -1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 3 \amp -3 \end{vmatrix} = \langle 0, -4, -4 \rangle
				</me>
				The equation of the plane using point <m>A(1, 0, 2)</m>:
				<me>0(x-1) - 4(y-0) - 4(z-2) = 0 \implies -4y - 4(z-2) = 0 \implies y + z = 2.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				An object's acceleration is given by
<me>\vec{a}(t)=\left\langle e^{2 t}+1,3 t^2+2,4\right\rangle</me>.
Its initial velocity is <m>\vec{v}(0)=\left\langle 0,1,0 \right\rangle</m> and its initial position is <m>\vec{r}(0)=\left\langle 2,0,1 \right\rangle</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find a vector equation <m>\vec{v}(t)</m> for the velocity of the object at time <m>t</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{v}(t) \amp = \int \vec{a}(t) \, dt = \langle \frac{e^{2t}}{2} + t + c_1, \frac{3t^3}{3} + 2t + c_2, 4t + c_3 \rangle \\
						\vec{v}(0) \amp = \langle 0, 1, 0 \rangle = \langle \frac{1}{2} + c_1, c_2, c_3 \rangle \\
						\implies c_1 \amp = -1/2, c_2 = 1, c_3 = 0 \\
						\vec{v}(t) \amp = \langle \frac{e^{2t}}{2} + t - \frac{1}{2}, t^3 + 2t + 1, 4t \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find a vector equation <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> for the position of the object at time <m>t</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}(t) \amp = \int \vec{v}(t) \, dt = \langle \frac{e^{2t}}{4} + \frac{t^2}{2} - \frac{t}{2} + D_1, \frac{t^4}{4} + t^2 + t + D_2, 2t^2 + D_3 \rangle \\
						\vec{r}(0) \amp = \langle 2, 0, 1 \rangle = \langle \frac{1}{4} + D_1, D_2, D_3 \rangle \\
						\implies D_1 \amp = 7/4, D_2 = 0, D_3 = 1 \\
						\vec{r}(t) \amp = \langle \frac{e^{2t}}{4} + \frac{t^2}{2} - \frac{t}{2} + \frac{7}{4}, \frac{t^4}{4} + t^2 + t, 2t^2 + 1 \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the speed of the particle at time <m>t</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The speed is the magnitude of the velocity vector:
					<me>|\vec{v}(t)| = \sqrt{\left(\frac{e^{2t}}{2} + t - \frac{1}{2}\right)^2 + (t^3 + 2t + 1)^2 + (4t)^2}.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find a vector function that parametrizes the curve of intersection of the cone <m>z=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> and the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=9</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>x = 3 \cos t</m> and <m>y = 3 \sin t</m> to satisfy <m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m>.
				Then <m>z = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2} = \sqrt{9} = 3</m>.
				The parametrization is:
				<me>\vec{r}(t) = \langle 3 \cos t, 3 \sin t, 3 \rangle.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle t^2, \sin (\pi t), t+2\right\rangle</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write down an integral that represents the length of the curve, with <m>1 \leq t \leq 3</m>. You do NOT need to solve the integral.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}\,'(t) \amp = \langle 2t, \pi \cos(\pi t), 1 \rangle \\
						|\vec{r}\,'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{(2t)^2 + (\pi \cos(\pi t))^2 + 1^2} = \sqrt{4t^2 + \pi^2 \cos^2(\pi t) + 1} \\
						L \amp = \int_1^3 \sqrt{4t^2 + \pi^2 \cos^2(\pi t) + 1} \, dt
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find a parametric equation for the tangent line to the curve at the point <m>(4,0,4)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The point <m>(4, 0, 4)</m> corresponds to <m>t = 2</m>.
					The direction vector is <m>\vec{r}\,'(2) = \langle 4, \pi \cos(2\pi), 1 \rangle = \langle 4, \pi, 1 \rangle</m>.
					The parametric equations are:
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						x \amp = 4 + 4t \\
						y \amp = 0 + \pi t \\
						z \amp = 4 + t
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the equation of the normal plane to the curve at the point <m>(4,0,4)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The normal vector is <m>\vec{n} = \vec{r}\,'(2) = \langle 4, \pi, 1 \rangle</m>.
					The equation is:
					<me>4(x-4) + \pi(y-0) + 1(z-4) = 0 \implies 4(x-4) + \pi y + z - 4 = 0.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
Let <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle 2 \sin (t), 2 \cos (t), 3 t\right\rangle</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit tangent vector, <m>\vec{T}(t)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}\,'(t) \amp = \langle 2 \cos t, -2 \sin t, 3 \rangle \\
						|\vec{r}\,'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{4 \cos^2 t + 4 \sin^2 t + 9} = \sqrt{13} \\
						\vec{T}(t) \amp = \langle \frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} \cos t, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} \sin t, \frac{3}{\sqrt{13}} \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit normal vector, <m>\vec{N}(t)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{T}\,'(t) \amp = \langle -\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} \sin t, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} \cos t, 0 \rangle \\
						|\vec{T}\,'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{\frac{4}{13} \sin^2 t + \frac{4}{13} \cos^2 t} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} \\
						\vec{N}(t) \amp = \langle -\sin t, -\cos t, 0 \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit binormal vector at <m>t=\pi</m>, that is, find <m>\vec{B}(\pi)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{T}(\pi) \amp = \langle -\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}}, 0, \frac{3}{\sqrt{13}} \rangle \\
						\vec{N}(\pi) \amp = \langle 0, 1, 0 \rangle \\
						\vec{B}(\pi) \amp = \vec{T}(\pi) \times \vec{N}(\pi) = \begin{vmatrix} \hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\ -2/\sqrt{13} \amp 0 \amp 3/\sqrt{13} \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{vmatrix} = \langle -\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}, 0, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the function <m>f(x, y)=e^{\sqrt{4 x^2-y}}</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the domain of the function.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We need <m>4x^2 - y \ge 0 \implies y \le 4x^2</m>.
					The domain is <m>\{(x, y) \mid y \le 4x^2\}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the domain of the function. Shade the region(s) that are part of the domain. Use dotted lines when sketching a curve that is NOT in the domain, and solid lines when sketching curves that are.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The domain is the region on and below the parabola <m>y = 4x^2</m>. The parabola itself is included (solid line).
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What is the range of the function?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The square root <m>\sqrt{4x^2 - y}</m> takes all values in <m>[0, \infty)</m>.
					Thus, <m>f(x, y) = e^{\text{something } \ge 0}</m> has range <m>[1, \infty)</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Let <m>f(x, y)=\frac{2 x^2 y^2+y^4}{3 x^3 y}</m>. Show that <m>\lim _{(x, y) \rightarrow(0,0)} f(x, y)</m> does not exist.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Along path 1 <m>y = x</m>:
				<me>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{2x^4 + x^4}{3x^4} = 1.</me>
				Along path 2 <m>y = 2x</m>:
				<me>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{2x^2(4x^2) + 16x^4}{3x^3(2x)} = \frac{24x^4}{6x^4} = 4.</me>
				Since limits are different, the limit does not exist.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>